Uh, I'm trying to set up here in such
a way oriented with my camera that

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.100
you don't see the, um, microphone.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.480
I hope that is the case.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.910
In fact, I will check on that right now.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.310
I think I can do that here,
if you bear very with me...

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.230
and yeah.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.820
Microphone is out of view.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:19.035
Also, uh, I'm going to do, I mentioned in
that previous video where I talked about

00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:24.825
the seven goals, uh, of language learning,
I'm going to go through each goal and

00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:27.345
talk about how we, in fact, learn words.

00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:32.865
For example, today I did a video about
how I learned Ukrainian, and I'm going

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:37.175
to continue doing videos about how
I learned these different languages.

00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:40.995
Um, I wasn't happy with the video
I did about learning Ukrainian

00:01:40.995 --> 00:01:45.135
because I know I have a number of
books at home in Vancouver, and

00:01:45.135 --> 00:01:46.305
I wanted to show these to you.

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:51.295
So I'm going to either use that
video or redo the video, uh, a month

00:01:51.315 --> 00:01:53.385
from now when I'm home in Vancouver.

00:01:54.135 --> 00:01:55.515
Uh, but I will be...

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:59.115
and so if you're interested in hearing
how I learned the different languages, let

00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:02.565
me know which languages you're interested
in hearing about and I will try to make

00:02:02.925 --> 00:02:08.009
videos about how I learned them, to the
what degree, in fact, I did learn them.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.790
So words, when we designed LingQ, it
was designed around the idea that to

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.410
me, the simplest measure of where you
are in the language is how many words

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.390
you know, and that the task of learning
languages is one of learning words.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.455
And I remember that, I remember that
when we learned French at school

00:02:29.905 --> 00:02:31.705
and I really couldn't speak French.

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:36.145
But, um, when I was, I was actually at
a football game, professional football,

00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:40.935
Montreal Alouettes, and I lost my ticket
and I was explaining to the, uh, usher

00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:45.720
in French and after 10 years of French,
all I could do is put together words.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:51.900
I couldn't use the verbs
correctly ... uh, you know, just words.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.140
And I think that the easiest
thing to acquire is words.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.820
And if we have enough words, we
actually can do a lot with those words

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:04.240
and that the refinement with, with
grammar and so forth comes later.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.655
So, and therefore, when we designed
LingQ, we built all kinds of things

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:12.345
into LingQ that measure how many words
you've read, how many words you've

00:03:12.345 --> 00:03:15.825
learned, how many words, you know,
how many words you've looked up.

00:03:16.215 --> 00:03:23.745
Uh, we have different highlighting to tell
you what stage you are with that word.

00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:29.965
So words, um, most of the words you
acquire, you're going to acquire

00:03:31.109 --> 00:03:33.899
subconsciously by being exposed to them.

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:39.839
And it's difficult to tell when certain
words will click in, uh, some you learn

00:03:39.839 --> 00:03:43.829
fast and some take forever to remember
some we remember, and then we forget

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.540
again, and then we remember them again.

00:03:45.839 --> 00:03:47.820
That's all fine.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.019
But initially you say, if you're
on LingQ and you've got a page

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:55.679
of text in a new language, let's
say Finnish for me or Icelandic

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:57.449
or something, everything is blue.

00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:02.925
And I have to look up every single
blue word and ideally I do it in

00:04:02.925 --> 00:04:07.875
sentence mode so I'm not having to
deal with too much content at once.

00:04:08.145 --> 00:04:11.505
And slowly, all of a sudden, each
one of these blue words, I looked

00:04:11.505 --> 00:04:13.215
them up and they are now all yellow.

00:04:13.695 --> 00:04:19.430
And because each language has a different
structure uh, the word order and so forth

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:24.050
may not be the same as English, but I can
kind of figure out what the sentence says.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.280
Very often there's a translation
underneath, into English in my case.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:34.820
Uh, and so I'm on my way to getting a
grasp of the language, I'm on my way

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:41.480
to acquiring words, the ultimate goal,
if we use again LingQ as the example is

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.650
that the whole lesson page will be white.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:45.780
That the word...

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.630
the blue words will have become
yellow words because I looked them

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.650
up and I mean, you know, I'm trying
to learn these words, but eventually

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.570
these words do become known.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:55.650
When they're known they're white.

00:04:55.979 --> 00:05:01.020
And I noticed that over months or so,
because any word, once I've learned

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.090
it and it's become white, it will show
up white in every subsequent lesson.

00:05:06.359 --> 00:05:10.409
And any word that went from blue to yellow
will be yellow in any subsequent lesson.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.569
So there's a gradual process of making
these pages lighter and lighter in colour.

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:19.640
Now, some people say, oh, and I
should also point out that the

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.600
only thing we can measure at
LingQ is your passive vocabulary.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.640
And I I've said this before in,
in videos, I'm a great believer in

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:31.160
the power of passive vocabulary.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.160
I'm a great believer in
input, in comprehension.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.020
So we, when we see a word in a
context and we understand that

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:44.460
word in that context at least in my
case, I consider that word known.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.470
It is known in that context.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.060
It is known passively.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.780
It doesn't mean that I can use
the word, but I'm on my way.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.650
And it may be that in another
context, I'll see that word

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.000
and I won't know what it means.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:02.415
And then I'll simply move it
back to unknow, to status one.

00:06:02.565 --> 00:06:07.305
It doesn't matter because the
process of acquiring words is largely

00:06:07.305 --> 00:06:12.645
subconscious and it's largely a process
of being exposed to these words.

00:06:13.095 --> 00:06:18.615
Now, some people will say, phrases are
more important than words, and I do save

00:06:18.615 --> 00:06:23.805
a lot of phrases, but you have to know the
meaning of the words inside the phrase.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:29.450
When you see phrases with words that
naturally belong together, you can then

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:33.440
save that phrase and try to remember
that phrase so that you have a handy

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.659
ready to use phrase with the way these
words are normally used in the language.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.010
But the fundamental
job is acquiring words.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:48.270
And if you don't need to look a word
up, LingQ will assume that you know

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:53.325
it, So another thing you often hear
people say is, well, you don't really

00:06:53.325 --> 00:07:00.075
need many words because, uh, 70% of
any context, you know, is gonna consist

00:07:00.075 --> 00:07:04.755
of 500, 800, 1000 most common words.

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:06.224
That's true.

00:07:06.554 --> 00:07:11.385
But if you only ever want to have very
limited conversations with people,

00:07:11.715 --> 00:07:15.615
if you don't ever want to understand
books or movies, maybe that's fine.

00:07:15.825 --> 00:07:19.455
But most people, certainly in my
case, when I learn a language,

00:07:19.755 --> 00:07:21.075
I want to understand movies.

00:07:21.195 --> 00:07:23.865
I want to have intelligent
conversations with people.

00:07:24.795 --> 00:07:30.135
To do all of that I have to have a large
passive vocabulary, not just a thousand

00:07:30.135 --> 00:07:33.435
words, 10,000 words, 20,000, 30,000.

00:07:33.795 --> 00:07:37.635
That enables me then to listen to
a variety of different content,

00:07:38.055 --> 00:07:42.855
podcasts, movies, and all of that
is helping the language come into

00:07:42.855 --> 00:07:48.090
me and most of all I am driven by
the desire to acquire more words.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.890
Now, some people like using spaced
repetition systems, I don't, because

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:57.239
I don't want to spend so much time
going through these decks of words.

00:07:57.719 --> 00:07:58.229
Also.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.690
I'm not a big fan of flashcards, but
other people do use them and swear

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.219
by them and think they're great.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:06.419
And so that's fine.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.690
I'm not against them, but
even at LingQ where we have

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.549
flashcards, different activities.

00:08:12.255 --> 00:08:17.325
I sometimes do them, but mostly
I just work with lists, uh, in

00:08:17.325 --> 00:08:22.005
every lesson behind every lesson
page, behind every sentence I can

00:08:22.005 --> 00:08:25.005
review the words from that page.

00:08:25.665 --> 00:08:29.175
I can review the LingQs, words
that I'm trying to learn.

00:08:29.205 --> 00:08:32.775
I can review words that
I have never seen before.

00:08:32.805 --> 00:08:33.555
Blue words.

00:08:33.765 --> 00:08:37.125
I can review words that I have
long since moved to known.

00:08:37.125 --> 00:08:38.865
I can review them if I want.

00:08:39.525 --> 00:08:41.195
I like seeing words in lists.

00:08:41.675 --> 00:08:44.954
Lists to me are better than
flashcards because you can

00:08:44.954 --> 00:08:47.324
see more of them more quickly.

00:08:47.564 --> 00:08:51.435
And to me, reviewing words is
just another form of exposure.

00:08:51.735 --> 00:08:55.365
It gives you another kind of
activity where you are being exposed

00:08:55.365 --> 00:08:59.354
to the language, and with lists
there are a number of advantages.

00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:03.105
You can go through the list
alphabetically so then you start to

00:09:03.105 --> 00:09:05.355
see words that have the same prefix.

00:09:05.715 --> 00:09:08.535
Very often you'll start to
see connections between words.

00:09:08.685 --> 00:09:12.675
You'll see that you have saved three, four
or five, six words that begin the same

00:09:12.675 --> 00:09:15.435
way and maybe share some common meaning.

00:09:15.525 --> 00:09:16.875
I find that very helpful.

00:09:17.565 --> 00:09:20.955
Uh, you can review your words
in lists based on frequency.

00:09:21.285 --> 00:09:26.495
And if you like, I'm often motivated to
move some of the words that I have saved,

00:09:27.045 --> 00:09:30.735
and I have a lot of them in all the
languages, I want to move them to known.

00:09:30.885 --> 00:09:36.375
So if I take my high-frequency words,
I'm more likely to find words there that

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:40.455
I actually already know, but I haven't
had a chance to move them to known.

00:09:40.695 --> 00:09:43.515
So I'll go in there and I'll
move a bunch of words to known.

00:09:43.695 --> 00:09:48.825
It's good for my known words total, and
also for my learned words total on LingQ.

00:09:49.725 --> 00:09:54.685
Another thing you can do with
lists is...yeah well, let's

00:09:54.704 --> 00:09:56.235
move on to another subject here.

00:09:56.265 --> 00:09:59.324
Words have different
functions in the sentence.

00:09:59.535 --> 00:10:05.265
So even without knowing, say, all of the
conjugations of verbs, at least we're

00:10:05.265 --> 00:10:08.685
aware that there are words that are verbs.

00:10:08.834 --> 00:10:10.875
There are words that are nouns.

00:10:10.875 --> 00:10:14.415
There are words that are adjectives,
adverbs are conjunctions, in

00:10:14.415 --> 00:10:15.735
other words connecting words.

00:10:16.214 --> 00:10:18.224
So we can tag for these.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.750
Um, and this is helpful because then you
can go to your vocabulary section and you

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.900
go through just verbs or just adjectives.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:32.340
And you can start to see things
that these adjectives or verbs

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.630
or whatever have in common.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.220
Another thing is it may be that
in some languages, you have

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.250
trouble with a third person of the
past tense you can tag for that.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:46.620
So you can look at the third person of
the past tense only as a list or you

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.490
might, if languages that have cases, you
can look at just the instrumental case,

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.260
let's say in Russian or Slavic languages.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:53.790
And you can just review those.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:59.400
So lists to my mind are more useful
in terms of reviewing for exposure

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:04.080
then flashcards, because I'm not
actually that interested in racking my

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.670
brain to try and remember something.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.330
I don't actually believe
that that's all that useful.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:15.690
So the main thing is acquiring words so
that I understand more things so that

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:20.370
when I'm listening to it, I find more
words that I recognize, even if I'm

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:24.900
fuzzy on the grammar, the words generally
speaking, the meaning is redundant.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:30.540
So even without having a full knowledge
of the grammar, you can kind of figure

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.880
out the meaning if you have the words.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:39.645
So goal number one to me is acquiring
words, acquiring them naturally

00:11:40.065 --> 00:11:44.025
varying your activity so that you
can sometimes get tired of reading

00:11:44.025 --> 00:11:46.305
and, and saving words and phrases.

00:11:46.785 --> 00:11:50.475
And so you can go through lists
of words for different reasons

00:11:50.475 --> 00:11:54.165
in order to maybe move the status
to known or whatever it might be.

00:11:55.335 --> 00:11:58.905
And so, as you progress in the
language and particularly when you get

00:11:58.905 --> 00:12:04.305
past sort of the first stage in the
language, because again, remember that

00:12:04.305 --> 00:12:06.535
frequency declines very, very quickly.

00:12:07.015 --> 00:12:10.574
So in an initial period, you're seeing
words quite often, you don't have to

00:12:10.574 --> 00:12:15.045
worry about aceing the most, the 500
most frequent words in the language.

00:12:15.135 --> 00:12:17.834
They're frequent, they will show
up, you needn't worry about them.

00:12:18.465 --> 00:12:21.765
And the frequency declines
very, very quickly.

00:12:22.035 --> 00:12:26.055
And so pretty soon we're into a
space, what I've called the sort

00:12:26.055 --> 00:12:31.665
of long, uh, you know, shaft of the
hockey stick, where it, we feel as

00:12:31.665 --> 00:12:32.625
if we're not making any progress.

00:12:33.525 --> 00:12:36.435
We're not seeing these words as
often as we used to, because the

00:12:36.435 --> 00:12:38.925
words we now need are less frequent.

00:12:39.435 --> 00:12:43.605
And that's where again, on LingQ,
where we keep track of how many

00:12:43.605 --> 00:12:46.185
words you've learned, how many words
you've known, how many LingQs you've

00:12:46.185 --> 00:12:47.535
created, how many words you've read.

00:12:47.925 --> 00:12:52.445
This tells you that you are being active,
that you are pursuing these words.

00:12:53.005 --> 00:12:55.620
And if you are pursuing these
words, you will improve.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:59.640
You will acquire the language because
to pursue these words, you've got

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.079
to be it listening and reading.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.540
If you're listening and
reading and pursuing words,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.010
the language is coming in you.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:10.410
So to summarize, number one goal in
language learning is to acquire words,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:15.959
keep that as your goal, keep that as your,
your major activity, be conscious of the

00:13:15.959 --> 00:13:17.699
fact that you are acquiring these words.

00:13:18.105 --> 00:13:21.105
Even though at times it seems
that you can't remember them.

00:13:21.285 --> 00:13:23.835
You can't remember when
you need to use them.

00:13:24.105 --> 00:13:25.935
You keep on forgetting their meaning.

00:13:26.295 --> 00:13:27.405
None of that matters.

00:13:27.495 --> 00:13:32.145
If you continue to pursue words,
you will eventually achieve your

00:13:32.145 --> 00:13:34.365
goal insofar as words are concerned.

00:13:34.575 --> 00:13:39.165
And your goal insofar as
achieving fluencies is concerned.

00:13:39.255 --> 00:13:40.515
So thank you for listening.

00:13:40.515 --> 00:13:44.955
That was the first in the series
on the seven most important

00:13:44.955 --> 00:13:46.275
goals in language learning.

00:13:46.755 --> 00:13:47.055
Bye for now.

